{
 "about": "The force and exponential windows of an impact test, sample by sample. Every implementation reproduces \"samples\" to \"tolerance\".",
 "tolerance": 1e-12,
 "definitions": {
  "force": "w[n] = 1 for n < W; (1 + cos(pi (n - W) / F)) / 2 for W <= n < W + F; 0 for n >= W + F; n from 0 at the frame's first sample, W the width and F the fall in samples. Named by fractions: W = floor(width * length + 0.5), F = floor(fall_fraction * W + 0.5), fall_fraction 0.1 unless given.",
  "exponential": "w[n] = exp(-n / tau), n from 0 at the frame's first sample, tau in samples = tau_s * sample_rate_hz. Named by its end value e (the value at n = length - 1): tau = (length - 1) / -ln(e); e = 1 is no decay.",
  "damping": "A mode windowed by exp(-t / tau_s) has its decay rate raised by 1 / tau_s, so its damping ratio reads high by about 1 / (2 pi f_n tau_s)."
 },
